WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.640
A high school student has identified three dwarf galaxies whose star formation patterns may resolve a long-standing puzzle about isolated systems. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 217. Julian Shapiro found three dwarf galaxies while still in high school. He was studying star formation rates near the spiral galaxy M-101. The galaxies now carry the name Shapiro I, II and III. Shapiro now studies astrophysics at the University of California, Berkeley. He works there with researcher

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.920
Elexay Filipenko. His paper appeared in the astrophysical journal. He earned a Davidson

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:40.480
Fellowship for the project. Archival telescope data made the fine possible without new observations.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.700
Shapiro said the work could inspire other young researchers. Filipenko called the high school

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:50.980
research remarkably sophisticated. Dwarf galaxies can contain as few as a thousand stars.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:56.460
They also reach up to several billion stars. They often form from material flung out during

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:57.940
larger galaxy collisions.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.900
Next up, NASA and the Department of Energy have signed a new agreement on nuclear power

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:03.420
for space.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.800
The memorandum of understanding was signed on October 8.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.840
Its full title is Accelerating American Leadership in Space Nuclear Power and Propulsion.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.820
The deal covers research, fuel production, testing, launch integration, and operations.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:19.900
It takes effect on November 1.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.180
It supports the Space Reactor 1 Freedom Mission set for 2028.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.040
NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman said the work prepares Fission-powered spacecraft.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.040
The Dragonfly mission to Titan will use a multi-mission radio-isotope thermal electric

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:34.580
generator.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.120
It will also carry 24 heater units under the new framework.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.320
Lunar reactor 1 is planned as a Fission-surface power system for future lunar bases.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.300
On another note, astronomers have spotted signs of a planet forming around a dying star.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:52.860
The observations show material ejected by the star assembling into a new world.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.180
The process resembles conditions in the early solar system.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:59.820
The findings suggest planets can form late in a star's life.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.780
A defunct Soviet satellite has broken apart in orbit once more.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.280
Experts call the repeated events part of a worrying trend in debris creation.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:12.540
The breakup adds to risks facing active spacecraft from old hardware.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.400
NASA has selected the Prima-Far Infrared Telescope to move forward in development.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.580
Prima stands for P-Robe Far Infrared Mission for Astrophysics.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.000
It carries a 1.8-meter mirror.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.000
The mission will be the first in a new class of telescopes.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.920
It will examine how the universe evolved through far infrared light.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:35.720
The European Space Agency's solar orbiter has traced the source of magnetic switchbacks

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:36.640
in the solar wind.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.480
The spacecraft passed through an S-shaped kink in the magnetic field.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:44.500
Particle fingerprinting inside the structure pointed back to the sun's surface.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.880
The data give fresh insight into how solar magnetism creates these features.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.940
The European Space Agency and the International Astronomical Union have signed an agreement

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.220
to protect dark skies.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.180
They met at the International Astronautical Congress.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.440
The partnership will develop ways to reduce satellite light and radio interference.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.100
More satellites have raised both reflected light and frequency issues for astronomers.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.640
The James Webb Space Telescope has detected the most distant fast radio burst yet recorded.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.040
The burst traveled more than 10 billion years to reach Earth.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.160
Web placed its host galaxy at a red shift of 2.148.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.100
The event sets a new record for the farthest known fast radio burst.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.740
South Korea launched its Nuri 2 rocket on October 7.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.720
The vehicle carried five neon-sat images and nine cube-sats.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.720
The flight advanced the country's orbital launch record.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.640
Voyager has a launch contract with SpaceX for a Starship mission.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.600
The agreement gives the project its ride to space.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.000
The United Launch Alliance Vulcan Rocket aims for a net October 29th return to flight.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.600
SpaceX has acquired a nationwide low-band spectrum license portfolio.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.260
The move supports Starlink becoming a major mobile carrier in the United States.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.460
The Gen 2 constellation will deliver high-speed broadband from orbit.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:05.840
The Copernicus Sentinel-3C satellite sent back its first images two weeks after launch.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.260
Two key instruments confirm the satellite is working well.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.660
It will monitor oceans, land, ice, and atmosphere.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.500
Japan's MMX mission will visit the Martian Moon Phobos.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.980
It will land twice and collect several tablespoons of material.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.660
Samples return to Earth in 2031.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.860
The material could clarify whether Phobos is a captured asteroid or an impact remnant.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.020
It may also contain dust blasted off Mars itself.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:36.300
The James Webb Space Telescope has examined extreme debris discs around young stars.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:41.980
cars. These discs contain large amounts of warm dust in rocky planet zones. A team compiled

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:48.020
21 such discs from Spitzer and Web Archives. The systems appear around only about 1% of

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:53.200
young stars. Julian Shapiro's work with the Shapiro galaxies relied solely on archival

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.860
data. He measured star formation rates in dwarf systems near M-101 while still in high school.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:05.240
The galaxy's challenge standard models of isolated galaxy evolution. Shapiro will continue the research

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:09.560
as an undergraduate at Berkeley. His paper shows public archives can yield

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:14.380
original cosmological insights. Dwarf galaxies act as building blocks during

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:19.340
larger galaxy assembly. A fast radio burst releases as much energy in milliseconds

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:24.220
as the sun produces over days. The signal reaches Earth as a faint radio whisper

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:28.720
after crossing more than 10 billion light years. Imagine the burst as a lighthouse

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.280
flash that left its galaxy when the universe was less than a quarter its

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:37.340
present age. By arrival, the wave has spread across a volume larger than the Milky Way's

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:42.640
stellar disk repeated a million times end-to-end. The wave passes through intergalactic plasma

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:47.400
that slows lower frequencies, creating a delay astronomers use as a distance ruler.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:53.560
Web's detection at redshift 2.148 places the burst when the universe was roughly 3 billion

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:59.880
years old. We can measure arrival time, polarization, and dispersion with growing precision. Yet the

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.400
Mechanism that concentrates such energy into a millisecond stays unknown.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.400
No model yet explains why some bursts repeat and others do not.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:13.120
No model yet explains why host galaxies range from quiet ellipticals to star-forming dwarfs.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.640
Before we go, keep an eye on the next steps for the prima telescope as it moves through

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:18.520
development reviews.

